---
import NnNav from './NnNav.astro';
import ClubLogo from './ClubLogo.astro';

// The header at the top of every Nightingale Nightmare page.
//
// **This replaces a navigation that sat in the page flow**, below the hero's call-to-action
// buttons, which is where "the smallest thing that works" put it. It worked in the sense
// that the links resolved; it failed in the sense that nobody looking at the page would
// call it a navigation. A header is the thing people already know how to use, and it is
// worth the extra element.
//
// ## Where it goes, and why that is `Base.astro`'s business rather than this file's
//
// It fills the layout's `masthead` slot, which renders **outside `<main>`**. Two things
// depend on that and neither is cosmetic: "Skip to content" points at `#main` and would
// otherwise skip *to* the navigation rather than past it, and `<header>` only maps to the
// `banner` landmark when it is not inside `main`. See the note in `Base.astro`.
//
// ## What the mockup did and did not supply
//
// **`docs/design/nn-entry-mockup.html` has no header and no navigation at all** — no
// `<nav>`, no links bar, no `aria-current`, and the club wordmark is a non-linked
// `<p class="clublock">` sitting as the first child of the hero. So there was no approved
// pattern to copy and this is not one. What the mockup does supply is the vocabulary this
// is built from: bone on the blood gradient, the yellow focus ring, the fixed 20px gutter,
// and a header that is part of the poster rather than a bar bolted above it. Recorded here
// because "as per the mockup" would be a claim the file does not support.
//
// ## No JavaScript, and no hamburger
//
// Three controls fit. A menu behind a toggle would need a script to open, and every form on
// this site is built to work without one — a navigation that fails with JavaScript off
// while the entry form beneath it still works would be an odd place to draw the line.
//
// **Sticky, and this is the second time it has been.** It was stuck, then unstuck by ADR-012
// for three recorded defects and 207px of a 568px phone, and ADR-014 sticks it back with each
// of the three paid for — the full accounting is at the head of the masthead section in
// `nn-theme.css`. The 207px is the number worth correcting rather than arguing with: it
// belonged to a bar of *two rows of links*, one appearing only beneath a year, which ADR-012
// itself replaced. What is stuck now is 62px on a laptop and about 110px of a 320px phone.
//
// **It sticks on `/nn/<year>/entry/complete/` too, where there are no links in it.** That is
// one rule rather than an exception to remember, and what stays there is a single 62px row
// carrying the wordmark. A page somebody reaches having just paid is short enough that the
// alternative — a class, a conditional and a second selector — costs more than the row does.
//
// ## The button lives here rather than in the navigation, and why
//
// At 320px the bar was three rows: the wordmark and the button on the first, the campaign links
// over the next two. Getting the button onto the wordmark's row means it has to be the
// wordmark's **sibling** — inside `<nav>` it could only ever be on the links' rows. So it is a
// child of this header, and `nn-theme.css` reorders it with one `order` at that width.
//
// The semantic cost is that it is not inside the navigation landmark. It is a call to action
// rather than a place to go, it is still inside the banner, and it is announced immediately
// after the campaign links — which is what somebody jumping by landmark would want anyway.

interface Props {
  /**
   * Whether the campaign links appear beside the wordmark.
   *
   * `false` on `/nn/<year>/entry/complete/`, which somebody reaches having just paid. Five ways to
   * wander off before reading what the club has recorded is not what that page is for, so it
   * keeps the wordmark for identity and drops the links. It is the only page that does.
   *
   * **Privacy goes with them, and that is the one entry worth a second thought.** Somebody who
   * has just handed over a medical note is plausibly the person most likely to want the notice —
   * but this page is the club's answer to "did that work", the notice is one tap away from every
   * other page in the campaign, and it is linked from the entry form they filled in on the way
   * here. A page with one job keeps one job.
   */
  nav?: boolean;

  /**
   * Whether this bar stays on screen on a narrow viewport.
   *
   * `true` everywhere except `/nn/2026/`, and the exception is arithmetic rather than taste.
   *
   * **Two pinned strips is the cost ADR-012 rejected, reached from the other end.** That record
   * unstuck this bar and published the reason — 207px of a 568px phone, 36% held permanently.
   * [ADR-014](../../../../docs/architecture/decisions/adr-014-the-bar-stays-and-the-notice-is-in-it.md)
   * stuck it back and corrected that figure as stale: it described a two-row bar that had since
   * been deleted, and the real cost was 109px. The bar is 136px now, and the consolidated page
   * adds a fixed entry bar at a 64px floor — about 200px, 35%, which is within seven pixels of
   * the number ADR-012 refused.
   *
   * **Only that page, because only that page has the second strip.** The other seven campaign
   * pages have no bottom bar and therefore no arithmetic problem, and ADR-014's requirement —
   * the navigation is reachable from anywhere — is untouched on every one of them. It survives
   * on `/nn/2026/` too, in the form that page actually has: a jump-nav to its own sections and
   * a fixed bar carrying the price and the way in.
   *
   * **Narrow only.** Above 860px the entry bar is `display: none` and there is one pinned
   * strip again, so the bar stays stuck exactly as ADR-014 decided.
   */
  sticky?: boolean;
}

const { nav = true, sticky = true } = Astro.props;

// **The button is a destination too, so it carries the marker on its own page.** Matched on
// the *shape* of the path rather than on the painted href, exactly as the bar's links are:
// `/nn/2027/` is the running's own page whatever the year, and the marker is therefore right
// before the Worker has painted anything.
const path = Astro.url.pathname.endsWith('/')
  ? Astro.url.pathname
  : `${Astro.url.pathname}/`;

const onTheRunning = /^\/nn\/\d{4}\/$/.test(path);
---

<header class:list={['nn-masthead', { 'nn-masthead-unstuck-narrow': !sticky }]}>
  <div class="nn-wrap">
    {
      /* **The club's wordmark, and the page's only accessible copy of it.** It used to sit
      in the hero on all six pages; leaving it there as well would give every page two
      links or images saying "Southville Running Club", which is one more than a screen
      reader user needs to hear. The hero's copy is removed rather than hidden.

      **It goes to `/`, the club, not to `/nn/`.** A wordmark in the top-left corner is the
      most conventional target on the web and what it conventionally means is *home* — the
      organisation's front door, not the section you are standing in. Pointing it at `/nn/`
      made it the fifth way to reach a page the bar's first link already offers, and left
      the campaign with no route out to the club at all: the cross-site banner's own mark is
      `display: none` here precisely because this one exists. So this is the way back, and
      the accessible name says so, exactly as the banner's does on the club's own pages.

      The name still carries the club's name because that is what the artwork says — not a
      description of it. `width` and `height` are on the element so the row does not reflow
      between the HTML arriving and the stylesheet landing.

      **It was `<img src="/logo.svg">` and is now inline `<svg>`.** The file it pointed at
      was `fill:#fff`, which is why it could only ever appear here: a white wordmark is
      bone on this hero and invisible everywhere else, so `/` and `/timing` had no logo at
      all. The mark is `currentColor` now and takes bone from `.theme-nn a` exactly as this
      link's text would, which is what let the club's own pages have the same wordmark in
      the club's own green. Nothing about how it looks here changes. */
    }
    <a class="nn-masthead-mark" href="/" aria-label="Southville Running Club, home">
      <ClubLogo class="nn-logo" width={176} labelled={false} />
    </a>

    {nav && <NnNav />}

    {
      /* **The one thing this site exists to do, and the label is painted from the entry
      window.** "Enter" on a button that does not let you enter is a small dishonesty on a
      site that is about to ask for money, so the Worker chooses between three pairs:

        open       Enter the race / Enter
        pre_open   Register interest / Interest    — the interest form is on that page
        closed     Race details / Details          — and that page says entries have closed

      **Two labels because 320px cannot hold the long one beside the wordmark**, and an
      `aria-label` carrying the long one so the accessible name does not change with the
      viewport. Each short label is a substring of its long one, which is what WCAG 2.5.3
      asks: what somebody says out loud has to be in what the machine reads.

      It ships `hidden` with `href=""`, like the two year links in the nav, so a database this
      Worker cannot reach leaves a bar of two evergreen links rather than a button into a year
      nobody confirmed. The shipped text is the neutral pair and is never seen. */
    }
    {
      nav && (
        <a
          class="nn-nav-cta"
          href=""
          data-nn-nav-cta
          hidden
          aria-label="Race details"
          aria-current={onTheRunning ? 'page' : undefined}
        >
          <span class="nn-nav-cta-long" data-nn-nav-cta-long>
            Race details
          </span>
          <span class="nn-nav-cta-short" data-nn-nav-cta-short>
            Details
          </span>
        </a>
      )
    }
  </div>
</header>
